{
  "abstract": "We study biplanar graphs with two nonadjacent vertices adjacent to every other vertex. Such vertices would copy one color in a nine-color construction when their common neighborhood is eight-chromatic. We prove that every biplanar graph of this form on at most seventeen vertices is eight-colorable. More precisely, the double cone over any eight-vertex-critical graph of order at most fifteen is nonbiplanar. It follows at every order that an eight-critical common neighborhood in a biplanar double cone must contain an independent triple. The proof first strengthens a clique-avoiding path restriction to uniqueness of a seven-clique in the critical common neighborhood. At order fifteen, a triangle-free complement degree argument and an exact small profile catalog handle independence at most two. An independent-triple deletion argument forces the other branch to one triangle-plus-isolates complement. Smaller orders close by Gallai decomposition, Hall matching and profile deficits. Seven remaining geometric premises have complete necessary face encodings and independently checked binary RUP proofs totaling about 1.05 MB; all new finite evidence is supplied in one portable supplement. The balanced double-cone exclusion is imported from earlier work with its literal identification checked. We do not claim an eighteen-vertex example, a sharp bound, or a solution of the general Earth–Moon problem.",
